<title>JAPAN: Japan oil chief sees stable prices despite Iraq.</title>
<headline>Japan oil chief sees stable prices despite Iraq.</headline>
<dateline>TOKYO 1996-12-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>The resumption of Iraqi crude exports is not expected to have a large impact on international crude oil prices, Yuji Idemitsu, chairman of the Petroleum Association of Japan, said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>&quot;Iraqi crude, expected to emerge in the markets from January, is not expected to greatly influence the market,&quot; Idemitsu told reporters.</p>
<p>Prices are expected to remain stable at current levels during the winter months in the northern hemisphere, he said.</p>
<p>Iraq began exporting oil for the first time in six years on December 16 from the Turkish Mediterranean port of Ceyhan under a deal with the United Nations whereby Baghdad will sell $2 billion worth of crude over six months for food and medicine.</p>
</text>
